# Guardrails
- Use targeted replace_in_file edits; never rewrite a >400-line file whole.
- Read narrowly: grep/search_codebase to locate a symbol first, then read only
  that +-30 line window. Never whole-read a large file when a range will do.
- Confirm edits with `git diff --stat` / `git diff <file>` instead of re-reading.
- Pipe verification output through `tail -n 40` / `grep`; only dump full logs on failure.
- Batch independent reads/searches/edits into one tool call.

# Git (standing instruction from user, 2026-09-29)
- The words **"commit" and "push" mean the SAME thing: stage everything, commit, and push
  to `origin`.** Never commit without pushing, never push without committing, and never stop
  at a local commit to ask whether to push.
- Write the commit message from the actual change; reference the issue numbers it addresses.

# Workflow
- Plan mode: explore the minimum, record the plan as `- [ ]` boxes in
  `memory-bank/activeContext.md` with file paths, one-line Potential Bugs, and a
  concrete Verification Step. Present plan, then wait.
- Act mode: read `memory-bank/activeContext.md` ACTIVE items only; execute unchecked
  items in order; mark `- [x]` only after the Verification Step passes.

# Frontend module layout (UPDATE 3.0)
- `src/app.js` is the LAST classic script and owns ONLY the `window.onload`
  bootstrap. Features live in `src/features/{telemetry,gear-sim,catch-log,station,auth}/`;
  shared primitives in `src/shared/`. Classic scripts, one global scope.
- Target <150 lines for feature/component files; data/type/contract files
  (`src/data/*`, `docs/CONTRACT*.md`) are EXEMPT.
- Every feature file opens with `// public: a(), b() — purpose`.
- Moving/splitting a big classic-script file: slice by exact line ranges with a
  byte-for-byte tiling assertion (never hand-transcribe), then add the new files to
  BOTH `index.html` (load order, `app.js` last) AND `sw.js` SHELL_FILES, and bump VERSION.

# Reading discipline
- Never whole-read `src/data/*.json` (`wdfw_rules.json` is thousands of lines),
  `docs/ARCHIVE*.md`, or `docs/CHANGELOG.md`: grep/search the specific key, then
  read only a ±30 line window around the match.
- One feature boundary per Act prompt (not a rigid "max 2 files" rule).

# Verification
- After frontend changes run `node sanity_pass.js --quiet` — it derives the script
  list from `index.html`, so newly added modules are auto syntax/HTTP-checked
  (`--quiet` prints only failures + the PASSED/FAILED summary).

# Context handoff
- Monitor context window usage; when it gets tight, finish the current logical step
  and prepare a handoff.
- Handoff: update `memory-bank/activeContext.md` + `memory-bank/progress.md`, then end
  the turn with a condensed carry-over block (current objective, last completed step,
  immediate next step). No task-spawning tool exists — the user restarts the session
  manually and reads `activeContext.md`.

# Output terseness
- No prose wind-up, filler, or hedging; deliver diffs directly.
- YAGNI: implement the minimum block that satisfies the verification step.
- Terseness does NOT waive the base rule: end with a concise summary + flag any
  non-obvious decision.

# Instruction ledger (chat asks are not in the repo)
- Any instruction that outlives the current turn gets a `- [ ]` box in
  `memory-bank/activeContext.md` IN THAT TURN — act mode included, because act-mode asks
  are the ones that evaporate (rod length was acknowledged twice and lost both times).
- At phase end run `python3 scripts/session_instructions.py` (~11 KB, not the multi-MB
  transcript) and reconcile every turn against what shipped. Unmatched items are either
  done next or marked open-by-design. Never read the full transcript to hunt for misses.

# Database (standing instruction from user)
- ALWAYS author DB changes as an idempotent timestamped migration under
  `supabase/migrations/`, then APPLY IT YOURSELF with
  `npx supabase db push --yes < /dev/null`. Never leave a migration unapplied and
  never ask the user to run it by hand — they are already logged in + linked.
- Then VERIFY against the live DB (read-only query) before reporting done.
- `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW` cannot rename a column (42P16): `drop view if exists`
  first, then `create view` (matches `src/services/schema.sql`).
- One-time data fixes are migrations too, guarded so they are no-ops on re-run
  (e.g. `where id = '…' and river_name is null`).

# Terminal safety
- NEVER run the same failing command more than twice consecutively; then STOP and
  summarize the error + ask for guidance.
- No blocking foreground servers in the tool window (background + redirect to /tmp).
- Do NOT write files via shell heredocs: they can mangle and exit 1 WITHOUT executing.
  Use the editor to write a temp file, then `cp` it into place.

# Memory (memory-bank/)
- `memory-bank/` is the front page: projectbrief, productContext, activeContext,
  systemPatterns, techContext, progress. A fact lives there ONCE and points at the
  deep doc — never fork the same fact into two files (that is how docs go stale).
- Append a concise 1-2 line entry to `docs/CHANGELOG.md` at each feature/phase end.
- New chat: read `memory-bank/activeContext.md` (ACTIVE items) + `memory-bank/progress.md`
  + the last ~20 lines of `docs/CHANGELOG.md`. Not full history.

# Docs map
- `README.md` = the **GitHub landing page** (scope, architecture, how to run). Refresh it **in the
  SAME commit** as any change to user-facing behavior, the waterbody list, or the run instructions —
  nothing else keeps it honest (it drifted: the docs table missed `LITERATURE.md`, and the intro
  claimed 5 rivers while 15 ship).
- `memory-bank/*` = orientation · `AGENTS.md` + `.clinerules` = rules
- `docs/SYMBOLS.md` = file -> public API index (find a function without opening files)
- `docs/CONTRACT.md` / `_REGIONS` / `_TECHNIQUE` / `_CATCH` = data + API contracts
- `docs/LITERATURE.md` = the studies behind the environment model (thresholds + provenance)
- `docs/ROADMAP.md` (Update 4.0) · `docs/ARCHIVE_UPDATE_3.0.md` (completed blueprint)
- `docs/ARCHIVE.md` + `docs/CHANGELOG.md` = history / audit trail

# Continuous improvement
- On a style/architecture/tool correction: ask to persist it to .clinerules.
